= Requirement Gathering

Scope is what the application actually does and does not do.
#flashcard("What is scope", "What the application does and does not do")

A Prototype is a quickly made version of the application for presentation purposes
#flashcard("What is a prototype", "A quickly made application to show the extent of the app")

When asking questions to clients many types
- Likert Scale (strongly agree to strongly disagree)
- Categorical
- True/False
- Closed question
- Open Ended

#flashcard("5 types of questions used in requirement gathering", "Likert Scale, Categorical, True false, Closed ended, Open ended")

#flashcard("What is the likert Scale", "Strongly Agree -> Strongly Disagree")

#flashcard("Difference between open-ended and closed questions", "Open-ended is subjective and leaves room for explanations, whereas closed questions have objective answers like name or age")

Interviews are a scale. At one end, structured interviews have a list of exact questions, which makes them easy to follow and compare but can lack depth because there are no follow-ups. At the other end, unstructured interviews have no set questions, which allows follow-ups for deeper understanding but needs a skilled interviewer and is hard to compare. Semi-structured interviews sit in the middle: there is a list of questions, but follow-ups are used to get deeper information.

#flashcard("What is the scale of structure of interviews", "Structured - all set beforehand with no follow-ups; semi-structured - set questions with follow-ups; unstructured - made up on the spot but can get very deep")

A user story is used to inform developers about a feature, a bug, or another requirement. It helps developers understand the feature and the exact requirements needed.

#flashcard("What is a user story", "A short description of who the user is, what they are trying to do, and why")
#flashcard("Why are user stories good", "Gives developers a clearer idea of what is required")